<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theophilos the Indian</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/364">364</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Theophilus Indus, the Ethiopian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theophilos the Indian was a 4th-century Heteroousian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, of the Arian school of Aëtius, who came from an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, perhaps <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socotra">Socotra</a>, the Maldives or an island at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, and fell in and out of favour at the court of the Roman emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a>. Ordained a deacon by Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, he was known as a healer and is said to have cured the empress Eusebia. Around 354 Constantius sent him to south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, where he is said to have converted the Himyarites and built three churches, one of them at Zafar. In about 356 the emperor asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">Ezana</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksum</a> to replace Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frumentius">Frumentius</a> with him, a request that was turned down.</p></div>
